    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This project investigates how disruptions in circadian rhythms impact cued and contextual fear memories in Siberian hamsters (Phodopus sungorus). While studies in these hamsters show that the loss of circadian timing impairs spatial and recognition memory, it was unclear whether this impairment is permanent or reversible. To assess this, we employed a 4-day fear conditioning protocol followed one week later by a disruptive phase shift (DPS) protocol to eliminate circadian rhythms in these hamsters. They were then assessed 7 days and 42 days following the end of the protocol for memory of the fear conditioning. \r\n\r\nOur results show that temporary loss of circadian rhythms reversibly blocks recall of previously formed traumatic memories. Contextual memories are wholly blocked while cued memories are partially suppressed. In the contextual memory test, all animals exhibited increased fear behavior after initial testing. While hamsters that remained entrained to a normal circadian rhythm showed increased fear behavior at all timepoints, hamsters that became permanently arrhythmic showed completely suppressed fear behavior (no significant difference compared to baseline) after becoming arrhythmic (p < 0.05). Animals that were arrhythmic for a period of time before re-entraining showed completely suppressed fear behavior (p < 0.05) while they were arrhythmic; after re-entraining, these animals showed increased fear behavior similar to the entrained animals. \r\n\r\nIn the cued test, all animals exhibited fear behavior at all timepoints tested, but animals demonstrated partially suppressed fear behavior when arrhythmic compared to when entrained (p < 0.05). This demonstrates that loss of rhythmicity likely is responsible for impairing recall of the traumatic fear conditioning. This work has clinical implications in disorders involving memory loss such as Alzheimer’s disease.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
